const takeaway = `
<p>Stripping works, and it is worth seeing work. <code>()</code>, <code>[]</code> and
<code>{}</code> are closed pairs wherever they appear, cutting one out cannot change
the answer, and whatever was outside it becomes adjacent in turn. Delete until nothing
changes; the string is valid exactly when nothing is left.</p>

<p>What it costs is the restart. Every deletion makes two characters adjacent that were
not adjacent before, including characters to the left of the cut, so the scan has to
begin again at position 0. That is a pass per deletion — <code>O(n²)</code>.</p>

<p>The stack removes the rescan by keeping the one fact the scan was rediscovering:
<strong>which opener is still waiting</strong>. When a closer arrives, the only opener it
is allowed to close is the most recent one nobody has closed yet, and the top of a stack
is that element by construction. So each character is read once, the check is a single
comparison, and the whole thing is <code>O(n)</code>.</p>

<p>That is the shape to recognise, not the bracket table. Whenever the thing you have to
match is <em>the most recent unmatched thing</em>, a stack is the structure that has it in
hand: <strong>Min Stack</strong> keeps the smallest value of the most recent unpopped
prefix, <strong>Daily Temperatures</strong> resolves each day against the most recent
warmer-than-nothing day still waiting, and evaluating an expression closes each operator
against the most recent operands pushed. Same question, same answer.</p>

<p>Two edges the examples hide. A closer arriving at an empty stack is a failure, not a
no-op — pop before comparing and an empty pop has to count as a mismatch. And a stack that
still holds openers at the end is also a failure: the string ran out before they did.</p>`;
